metal2vulkan: a copy in compares the caller's bytes with their digest, not with the buffer's own memory (Scion)

Upstream:  metal2vulkan, https://github.com/steelbrain/metal2vulkan, at 720ef1ce9ae2a2068999a8675717418b9416283e, after
           AdrenoMetal's 16 patches to it and Scion's 0001 to 0008; tools/metal/mk-metal2vulkan.sh applies them in that order,
           then this.
Licence:   GPL-2.0-or-later OR LGPL-3.0-or-later, metal2vulkan's (its LICENSE); this patch is a change to it and under the same
           terms. Nothing of it is in Scion's MIT or APSL files.
Author:    the HakiOS authors (Scion), 14 November 2026.

Why. A no-copy buffer the device cannot import is copied in before every command buffer that uses it (0002), 4 KiB at a time
where the caller's bytes and the buffer's own memory differ, and the chunk's digest is then taken of the buffer's own memory
(0006). On PanVK on the Galaxy S9's Mali that memory is write-combined (its one host-visible type that is not an import), and
reading it back is slow: the window server on Metal spent 33 to 49 ms a commit in host_sync::copy_in for 0.6 to 1.2 MB a
commit (docs/metal.md, phase 6; 180 of 250 samples of its display thread in Buffer::copy_host_bytes).

What. A copy in takes the digest of each 4 KiB of the caller's bytes, which are cached memory, and copies the chunk only where
it differs from the digest recorded when the two last agreed (0006's, kept at each copy in and copy out); it records the new
digest. The buffer's own memory is written, never read. A buffer copied in for the first time takes every chunk. A chunk the
caller did not change since is left as the buffer's own memory has it, so a GPU write not yet copied out is no longer
overwritten with the caller's older bytes before it is.

--- a/crates/metal2vulkan-runtime/src/buffer.rs
+++ b/crates/metal2vulkan-runtime/src/buffer.rs
@@ -2074,8 +2074,10 @@
         let mut synced = lock(&copied.synced);
         let chunks = length.div_ceil(CHUNK);
         // Never synced: nothing the GPU wrote can be told from the caller's bytes, so a copy out
-        // gives back nothing (a copy in always comes first, before a command buffer's first batch).
-        if synced.len() != chunks {
+        // gives back nothing (a copy in always comes first, before a command buffer's first batch),
+        // and a copy in takes every chunk, whatever the digests say.
+        let fresh = synced.len() != chunks;
+        if fresh {
             if !in_ {
                 return (Vec::new(), 0);
             }
@@ -2085,6 +2087,23 @@
         while at < length {
             let end = (at + CHUNK).min(length);
             let chunk = at / CHUNK;
+            // A copy in takes a chunk only where the caller's bytes changed since they last agreed
+            // with the buffer's own memory (Scion, 0009): their digest against the one recorded
+            // then, read from the caller's cached memory alone. The buffer's own memory is
+            // write-combined on a device without coherent cached memory (PanVK on the Mali),
+            // where reading it back to compare cost the window server 33 to 49 ms a commit.
+            let theirs = in_.then(|| {
+                // SAFETY: the caller's bytes hold `length` bytes from their start (the no-copy
+                // contract), which no command reads or writes now (a copy in runs before the
+                // batch is submitted).
+                chunk_digest(unsafe { std::slice::from_raw_parts(caller.add(at), end - at) })
+            });
+            if let Some(digest) = theirs {
+                if !fresh && digest == synced[chunk] {
+                    at = end;
+                    continue;
+                }
+            }
             if !in_ {
                 // SAFETY: the buffer's own memory holds `length` bytes from its start, which no
                 // command writes now (a copy out runs after the work completed).
@@ -2111,7 +2130,7 @@
                     ),
                 }
             };
-            if from != to {
+            if in_ || from != to {
                 to.copy_from_slice(from);
                 copied_bytes += (end - at) as u64;
                 let offset = span.offset + at as u64;
@@ -2124,8 +2143,8 @@
                     }),
                 }
             }
-            if in_ {
-                synced[chunk] = chunk_digest(to);
+            if let Some(digest) = theirs {
+                synced[chunk] = digest;
             }
             at = end;
         }
